{"id":"f753356251a9-0029","source":"references/pptset/【加赠】700页逻辑图形 (1).pptx","sourcePage":29,"slideXmlSha256":"629da37efd144e1967c71a0b971a5f598d47fee5dc615149a91a4fb6d0c7c254","slots":[{"anchor":"s305-s304","text":"Customer\nRevenue","box":[0.1466,0.4737,0.0794,0.0636]},{"anchor":"s310-s309","text":"Support\nCosts","box":[0.3034,0.4737,0.0794,0.0636]},{"anchor":"s315-s314","text":"Average length of relationship","box":[0.4601,0.4598,0.0794,0.0916]},{"anchor":"s320-s319","text":"Support\nCosts","box":[0.6169,0.4737,0.0794,0.0636]},{"anchor":"s325-s324","text":"Customer Lifetime Value\nCLV","box":[0.7737,0.446,0.0794,0.1197]},{"anchor":"s339","text":"The purpose of the customer lifetime value metric is to assess the financial value of each customer. Don Peppers and Martha Rogers are quoted as saying, “some customers are more equal than others.”","box":[0.1876,0.2296,0.6248,0.0636]},{"anchor":"s360","text":"Customer lifetime value differs from customer profitability or CP (the difference between the revenues and the costs associated with the customer relationship during a specified period) in that CP measures the past and CLV looks forward. As such, CLV can be more useful in shaping managers’ decisions but is much more difficult to quantify. While quantifying CP is a matter of carefully reporting and summarizing the results of past activity, quantifying CLV involves forecasting future activity.","box":[0.1301,0.7179,0.7397,0.1197]},{"anchor":"s2","text":"How to Calculate Customer Lifetime Value CLV?","box":[0.0283,0.0378,0.9417,0.0673]}],"warnings":[],"stats":{"objects":62,"textBlocks":8},"reconstructedSlots":[{"anchor":"text-0","text":"Customer","box":[0.15469327116285767,0.4820578115957755,0.06314242445145989,0.023150013111255786],"origin":[0.15469327116285767,0.5005777994791667],"font":"DejaVuSans","size":12.50100040435791,"resolvedFamily":"DejaVu Sans"},{"anchor":"text-1","text":"Revenue","box":[0.15791198562890546,0.5098263210720486,0.056709700266295036,0.0231500